Document: 1 EXHIBIT 10.5

Findings:
[{"id":"f-indemn-cc763401","ruleId":"INDEMN","ruleTitle":"Indemnification by Vendor","severity":"critical","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"§ 8 INDEMNIFICATION.","rationale":"Section 8.1 gives us (Corio) and our Customers a full defend-and-indemnify from Commerce One for third-party claims that the Software infringes patent, copyright, trademark or trade secret rights, with standard procedure (prompt notice, tender of control, cooperation at Vendor's expense, right to join with own counsel) and the usual remedy ladder (procure rights, replace/modify, or terminate with pro-rated refund of fees paid). The Section 8.2 carve-outs are customary (use outside the Documentation; modification by a third party) and are no broader than the exclusions the fallback tolerates. There is no reciprocal Corio indemnity for the Vendor's software, so the walk-away scenario (one-way Customer indemnity) does not arise. Section 9 expressly excludes \"LIABILITY ARISING UNDER SECTION 8\" from both the 12-month fee cap and the consequential-damages waiver, so the Vendor indemnity is uncapped — better than the fallback's 3x super-cap. The clause therefore satisfies the fallback and substantially reaches the preferred position on IP. Noted but not flagged: the indemnity does not extend to Vendor's breach of applicable law, unauthorised access to Corio data, or gross negligence/wilful misconduct, which are preferred-position add-ons rather than material failures of the fallback.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-assign-50c3f58d","ruleId":"ASSIGN","ruleTitle":"Assignment and change of control","severity":"high","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"§ 14 MISCELLANEOUS.","rationale":"Section 14.1 is a mutual assignment clause that expressly permits either party — including us (Corio) — to assign the Agreement without consent in connection with a merger, acquisition or sale of all or substantially all assets related to the Agreement, so the M&A/successor carve-out the playbook requires is present and the walk-away case (blanket prohibition with no M&A carve-out) does not arise. Sections 11.2 and 11.3, and the Agreement generally, contain no change-of-control termination or re-pricing right for Commerce One (no \"change of control\" or \"deemed an assignment\" language anywhere in the document), so the second key risk is also absent. The clause therefore sits at the fallback (mutual successor assignment right, no notice condition even required) and largely at the preferred position. Two sub-preferred points, noted rather than flagged: there is no express carve-out allowing assignment to an affiliate without consent, and consent for other assignments is not qualified by a \"not to be unreasonably withheld\" standard; the direct-competitor consent requirement is reciprocal and mirrors the protection the playbook itself seeks against vendor assignments. These are minor gaps against preferred wording and do not defeat the fallback, so no redline is proposed.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-exclusivity-668b090f","ruleId":"EXCLUSIVITY","ruleTitle":"Exclusivity obligations binding Customer","severity":"high","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"SALES AND MARKETING COOPERATION","rationale":"We reviewed the Grant of Rights (Section 2), Shared Resources (Section 13), Exhibit B pricing/revenue sharing and Exhibit D sales and marketing cooperation. No provision obliges Corio to source exclusively from Commerce One, to purchase all of its requirements from Commerce One, or to refrain from licensing or hosting competing procurement/e-commerce software; all licences granted to Corio are expressly \"nonexclusive\" (running in Commerce One's favour as licensor, which is unobjectionable), and the Exhibit D cooperation commitments (relationship managers, joint marketing, marketing fund, initial customers) are stated to be \"non-binding\" and do not create any preferred-partner or sole-supplier restriction. Section 2.9's reference to future products is permissive and subject to terms \"as the parties may agree\", so it imposes no forward purchasing commitment. This meets the preferred position (no exclusivity or sole-supplier obligation on the customer), so no redline is required. We also note Section 2.4 (Commerce One must refer Corio Customers to Corio) and the MarketSite revenue share operate in our favour and are not flagged.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-ld-97567a12","ruleId":"LD","ruleTitle":"Liquidated damages and penalties payable by Customer","severity":"high","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"§ 4 FEES.","rationale":"The Agreement contains no liquidated damages, penalty, early-termination fee or minimum/shortfall payment obligation on our side. Fees payable by Corio (Sections 4.1–4.2 and Exhibit B/Payment Terms) are ordinary license, maintenance and revenue-share amounts, and Section 11 permits termination on breach or non-renewal with no termination charge or accelerated fee; Section 11.3 only continues fees for continuing use/support that we elect to receive. There is likewise no late-payment interest or penalty provision. The audit true-up in Section 4.2 (payment of a shortfall plus audit costs on a >5% underpayment) is a cost-recovery mechanic, not a liquidated damages or penalty amount. This meets the preferred position of no liquidated damages or penalties payable by the Customer, so no redline is proposed.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-noncompete-8fe4ffc2","ruleId":"NONCOMPETE","ruleTitle":"Non-compete restrictions on Customer","severity":"high","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"§ 3 2. GRANT OF RIGHTS.","rationale":"No non-compete or competing-product covenant binds Corio anywhere in the Agreement. Section 2.7 (p0042) is a conventional license-scope/reverse-engineering restriction, not a restraint on our business; Section 13.6 (p0117) expressly preserves each party's right to develop independently, and Section 14.8 (p0131) confirms Corio solely directs and may subcontract the Corio Services. The Territory (North America, expandable under Section 2.10) and Corio Market Segment (<$1B customers, with carve-outs in Section 1.3) are limits on the scope of the licenses granted to us rather than prohibitions on the businesses we may operate or the products we may buy or build, so they do not amount to a field-of-use non-compete on Customer. This meets the preferred position; no redline needed.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-warranty-78977b85","ruleId":"WARRANTY","ruleTitle":"Performance warranty and duration","severity":"high","status":"deviation","sectionRef":"§ 8 7 WARRANTIES AND DISCLAIMER.","rationale":"Section 7.3 gives a real conformance-to-Documentation warranty plus a no-malicious-code warranty, and Section 7.3/7.4 remedies (correction, replacement, and refund of prepaid fees on termination) satisfy the repair/replace/refund requirement; the implied-warranty disclaimer (p0084) does not purport to override these express warranties. The failing material term is duration: sixty (60) days measured from the Effective Date, with an absolute cut-off of December 31, 1999, is below the 90-day software fallback, and because Acceptance Testing may itself run to December 31, 1999 under Section 3.1, the warranty could lapse at or before acceptance — leaving effectively no conformance warranty. Section 11.2 (post-Warranty-Period spec compliance as a material condition) and the maintenance obligations in Section 5.3 mitigate but do not supply a warranty remedy. We therefore propose 12 months from acceptance (preferred), with 90 days from acceptance as our fallback. Absence of a professional-and-workmanlike standard for the professional/support services is noted but not separately flagged.","proposal":"Extend the software warranty to 12 months, delete the fixed 1999 expiry, and add a professional and workmanlike Services warranty with a 90-day Services period from performance, express repair remedy and compliance with laws.","verification":"repaired"},{"id":"f-audit-5a809242","ruleId":"AUDIT","ruleTitle":"Audit rights against Customer","severity":"medium","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"§ 4 FEES.","rationale":"Section 4.4 is the only audit/inspection right binding us (Corio, as licensee/payor), and it already tracks the preferred position on every material term: frequency is capped at no more than once each calendar year; the audit is conducted by an independent certified public accountant reasonably acceptable to us; it must occur at reasonable times; Commerce One bears all costs and expenses unless an underpayment of more than five percent (5%) is revealed (better than the 10% fallback trigger); and the right extends only to books of accounts and records for verifying amounts payable — there is no right of direct or remote access to our systems, so no walk-away issue. The only sub-element short of preferred is that notice is expressed as \"reasonable notice\" rather than a fixed 30 (or 15) days; notice is nonetheless required, so audits cannot be unannounced. We note this as a drafting nicety rather than a deviation and do not propose a redline.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-govlaw-016810da","ruleId":"GOVLAW","ruleTitle":"Governing law and venue","severity":"medium","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"§ 14 MISCELLANEOUS.","rationale":"Section 14.3 selects California law, which is expressly within the accepted list, and seats dispute resolution in San Francisco County, California — a domestic US forum with award enforcement available in the California courts. This sits at the playbook fallback (California law and forum) rather than the preferred position (New York law with exclusive jurisdiction of the state and federal courts in New York County), so it is acceptable and not redlined. Two sub-elements fall short of the preferred wording but are not material failures: disputes go to mandatory single-arbitrator AAA arbitration rather than directly to court, and the venue for enforcement is San Francisco rather than New York County. Neither triggers the walk-away test, which is limited to a vendor home jurisdiction outside the accepted list with exclusive foreign venue or arbitration seated abroad; the seat here is domestic and California is not an off-list jurisdiction. Section 12 (source code escrow) refers disputes back to Section 14.3, so the same accepted forum governs throughout, and no competing jurisdiction clause appears elsewhere in the Agreement.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-ip-598bd48e","ruleId":"IP","ruleTitle":"Ownership of deliverables and Customer Data","severity":"medium","status":"deviation","sectionRef":"§ 13 SHARED RESOURCES.","rationale":"Section 13.5 starts from the right place for us: Developments (including interface coding created solely by Corio) are owned by Corio and Commerce One assigns any claim of ownership to us, while Commerce One keeps its pre-existing materials and the Software (Section 2.8) — that matches the preferred position. The carve-out, however, flips ownership of work we create and fund to Commerce One whenever it supports those Developments on an ongoing basis, with no licence back to us. That is an assignment of our IP to the vendor with no right to exploit, which is walk-away territory under the rule and fails the fallback (no perpetual, irrevocable, royalty-free licence). Our redline simply deletes the carve-out, leaving Commerce One's background IP protected by the opening proviso. Noted but not flagged: ownership of enhancements to the Software itself is left to future negotiation (Vendor owns its platform, so this is acceptable), and the agreement contains no express customer-data ownership sentence, which is a minor gap in this hosting arrangement.","proposal":"Delete the carve-out that gives Commerce One ownership of Developments created solely by Corio where Commerce One supports them on an ongoing basis.","verification":"fail"},{"id":"f-mfn-779a84a2","ruleId":"MFN","ruleTitle":"Most-favoured-nation obligations burdening Customer","severity":"medium","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"§ 4 FEES.","rationale":"Searches for MFN/price-parity language (\"most favored\", \"no less favorable\", \"favorable\", \"lowest\", \"best price\", \"similarly situated\", \"preferential\", \"discount\") returned nothing, and a review of the Fees section (4.1-4.4), Exhibit B Pricing and Revenue Sharing Fees, Payment Terms and Exhibit D (Sales and Marketing Cooperation) shows only fixed fees, a stated revenue share and time-and-materials implementation rates. There is no obligation on Corio (our party) to extend Commerce One terms no less favourable than those given to any third party, or to match third-party offers. This meets the preferred position of no MFN obligation on the Customer, so no redline is required.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-mincommit-bd337a62","ruleId":"MINCOMMIT","ruleTitle":"Minimum purchase commitments and volume restrictions on Customer","severity":"medium","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"REVENUE SHARING FEES:","rationale":"The Agreement imposes no minimum purchase commitment, take-or-pay obligation, or volume cap on us (Corio, the licensee). Our recurring payment to Commerce One is a pure revenue share — a percentage of Application Management Revenue actually received from Corio Customers (p0157), accruing only once a Corio Customer \"begins live operations\" (p0160) — with no floor, guarantee, or shortfall charge. Implementation fees are time-and-materials against an agreed Statement of Work with a not-to-exceed cap (p0161), i.e., ordered services only. Annual maintenance and support is expressly conditional on our election to keep paying (\"provided that Corio continues to pay the annual Maintenance and Support fees,\" p0055), so we can exit that spend without penalty rather than being locked into five years of fees. Exhibit D's sales and marketing items, including the two-customer target, are stated to be \"non-binding\" and commercially-reasonable-efforts only (p0198, p0205). This satisfies the preferred position: fees are for usage or ordered services only. Two minor points noted but not flagged: (i) the sole \"minimum\" reference (p0158) is a metering convention — a 40 Authorized Software User floor per Customer applied when drawing down the initial 3000 licenses granted to us — which affects how quickly that royalty-free pool is consumed rather than obliging us to buy anything; and (ii) the only \"shortfall\" language is the reciprocal audit adjustment in §4.4 (p0059), which is a true-up (\"the appropriate party shall promptly pay the amount of such shortfall or refund such overpayment\") and not a penalty. No redline required.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-nosolicit-377d1af6","ruleId":"NOSOLICIT","ruleTitle":"Non-solicitation of employees binding Customer","severity":"medium","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"§ 14 MISCELLANEOUS.","rationale":"Searches for \"solicit\", \"hire\", \"employ\", \"induce\" and \"recruit\" return no hiring restriction anywhere in the Agreement. The Miscellaneous section (14.1–14.10) contains no restrictive covenant, Section 10 (Confidentiality) is limited to use of Confidential Information under the separate NDA, and the only personnel-related provisions — Section 13 (Shared Resources, which addresses Employee NDA/Invention Agreements and ownership of Developments) and Exhibit D, Section 5 (each party assigning a sales/marketing employee) — impose no non-solicitation or no-hire obligation on Corio. The integration clause confirms no other document within the four corners imposes such a covenant. This meets the preferred position (no non-solicit binding the Customer), so no redline is warranted; the absence of any restriction benefits us.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-renewal-bf109f2c","ruleId":"RENEWAL","ruleTitle":"Auto-renewal and non-renewal notice window","severity":"medium","status":"compliant","sectionRef":"§ 11 TERM AND TERMINATION.","rationale":"Section 11.1 provides automatic renewals of one (1) year (not multi-year) and a non-renewal notice window of only thirty (30) days exercisable by either party, so Corio as customer can exit each renewal on 30 days' notice. That meets the preferred position's material terms on renewal term length and notice window (well inside the fallback ≤60 days and far from the ≥90-day walk-away). Two preferred sub-elements are absent but are minor: there is no Commerce One reminder 60 days before renewal, and no express cap on renewal-period price increases — the Maintenance and Support and MarketSite.net fees are fixed by Exhibit B pricing tiers (\"Year 2-5, and beyond\"), so no unilateral uplift right is granted to Commerce One in any event. No redline warranted.","verification":"pass"},{"id":"f-transition-b2f8092e","ruleId":"TRANSITION","ruleTitle":"Post-termination transition assistance and data return","severity":"medium","status":"missing","sectionRef":"§ 11 TERM AND TERMINATION.","rationale":"The Agreement has no exit/transition clause. Section 11.3 preserves Corio's right to serve then-existing Customers and Commerce One's ongoing support obligation (and Section 12 provides source-code escrow), but neither obliges Commerce One to provide transition assistance on wind-down, and the only data-related term lets each party \"return or destroy\" the other's Confidential Information at its own election, with no format, no 30-day return window and no deletion certification. That falls short of the fallback (90 days assistance plus data export within 30 days) and edges toward the walk-away case of no data return. Inserted new Section 11.4 at the preferred position (six months' assistance at then-current rates, return within 30 days in machine-readable format, then certified deletion), expressly without limiting Corio's surviving rights under Section 11.3; a 90-day assistance period is an acceptable fallback.","proposal":"Insert Section 11.4 requiring Commerce One to provide transition assistance and return Corio data on exit","verification":"fail"},{"id":"f-insurance-d0b3ce78","ruleId":"INSURANCE","ruleTitle":"Vendor insurance","severity":"low","status":"missing","sectionRef":"§ 14 MISCELLANEOUS.","rationale":"The Agreement contains no insurance clause: the outline shows no insurance heading, the only textual hit for \"insurance\" is an unrelated revenue deduction in the definitions (p0012), and neither Miscellaneous (Section 14) nor Shared Resources (Section 13) imposes any coverage obligation. Commerce One is the vendor here (Corio is the licensee/hosting customer): it supplies the Software and MarketSite service and provides operations, consulting and engineering personnel who work alongside Corio personnel and touch Corio's hosted environment and data, so this is squarely a services/hosting vendor for which the playbook expects coverage. Absence of any insurance for a vendor with access to our data is walk-away territory, so we inserted a short covenant at the preferred levels (CGL 1M/2M, E&O 2M, cyber 5M, workers' compensation, certificates on request) in Section 14, noting the E&O 1M / cyber 2M fallback is available. Numbering of the new clause can be conformed on execution.","proposal":"Insert vendor insurance covenant in Section 14 (Miscellaneous)","verification":"repaired"},{"id":"f-license-2141e24b","ruleId":"LICENSE","ruleTitle":"Licence grant scope","severity":"low","status":"deviation","sectionRef":"§ 3 2. GRANT OF RIGHTS.","rationale":"The Section 2 grants run to Corio alone. Revocability is not a problem: Section 2.1 is expressly \"perpetual and irrevocable\" and Section 2.2 is \"fully paid up, perpetual\", and transferability meets the fallback because Section 14.1 permits assignment without consent on a merger, acquisition or sale of substantially all related assets (subject only to a direct-competitor consent carve-out). However, no provision extends the licence to our affiliates, there is no defined \"Affiliate\" and no written-notice mechanism to add them; Section 2.2 is confined to \"Corio's internal business operations\". Contractor use is addressed only indirectly (Corio Servers may be \"owned or operated by or for Corio\"). Affiliate coverage is a material term of both the preferred and fallback positions, so the grant fails the fallback on that element. Our minimal edit adds affiliates and contractors acting on our behalf to the internal use licence and leaves the rest of the grant structure, including the unlimited Customer sublicence, untouched.","proposal":"Add affiliates and contractors to the Section 2.2 internal use licence.","verification":"fail"},{"id":"f-lol-cap-90da358d","ruleId":"LOL-CAP","ruleTitle":"Limitation of liability — cap, mutuality and carve-outs","severity":"low","status":"deviation","sectionRef":"§ 9 LIMITATION OF LIABILITY.","rationale":"Section 9 caps both parties, but the basis is not 12 months' fees: it is limited to amounts \"ACTUALLY PAID BY CORIO ... FOR THE TRANSACTION WHICH THE LIABILITY RELATES TO\", a per-transaction slice of fees that can fall well below even six months' fees and so fails the fallback on cap size (and risks walk-away territory for Commerce One's exposure). Carve-outs are also short of the fallback: only Section 8 (Commerce One's IP indemnity) sits outside the cap, while breach of the confidentiality obligation in Section 10, gross negligence, wilful misconduct and fraud are all capped. The mutual exclusion of indirect/consequential damages is acceptable. Our redline moves the cap to the preferred basis (greater of 12 months' fees paid or payable and USD 1,000,000) and adds Section 10, gross negligence, wilful misconduct and fraud as uncapped carve-outs, keeping the existing sentence structure. We did not add the \"payment obligations are not damages\" clarification, which is a minor sub-element rather than a separate deviation.","proposal":"Cap set at the greater of fees paid or payable in the 12 months preceding the claim and USD 1,000,000, with uncapped carve-outs and a payment-obligations clarification.","verification":"fail"},{"id":"f-t4c-e8e1264b","ruleId":"T4C","ruleTitle":"Termination for convenience","severity":"low","status":"deviation","sectionRef":"§ 11 TERM AND TERMINATION.","rationale":"Section 11 gives Corio (our party, the licensee) no right to terminate for convenience during the five-year initial term: the only exits are non-renewal at the end of a term, material breach after a 30-day cure period, and insolvency. Absence of any customer convenience right in a multi-year term is walk-away territory under this rule, so it fails both the preferred (30 days' notice with pro-rata refund) and fallback (60–90 days' notice) positions. Commerce One likewise has no convenience right, which is consistent with the playbook and left unchanged. Redline inserts a new Section 11.4 giving Corio a 30-day convenience termination with refund of prepaid fees and confirming Commerce One has no mid-term convenience right; 60 days' notice is an acceptable fallback.","proposal":"Add a Customer (Corio) termination-for-convenience right on 30 days' notice with refund of prepaid fees and no Vendor mid-term convenience right","verification":"fail"}]
